7.3  Images, layers and overlayfs

TL;DR
  - An image is a stack of read-only layers, one per build step, plus some
    settings (command, environment). Layers are shared between images.
  - Running it adds a thin writable layer on top. overlayfs merges them into one
    root filesystem: you see the top-most version of each file.
  - Data you want to keep belongs in a volume or a bind mount, outside the
    container's layers.

      docker history IMAGE           # its layers and their sizes
      docker system df               # space used by images, containers, volumes
      docker run -v data:/var/lib/postgresql/data postgres

On your machine
  Here      no container or virtual machine detected
  Isolated  none: the host's namespaces (pid, user, uts, ipc, cgroup, time)
  Can run   VMs with KVM (/dev/kvm) · containers with Docker
  Overlays  1 overlay mount: container layers, usually

Pitfalls
  - Deleting a file in a later Dockerfile step doesn't shrink the image: the
    earlier layer still holds it. Download, use and delete in one RUN.
  - Changing an early step rebuilds every step after it: copy dependency lists
    and install them before copying the source code.
  - A tag such as postgres:16 moves when it's updated; a digest (@sha256:…)
    never does.
  - Images and stopped containers pile up: docker system df, then prune what you
    don't need.

Deep dive

  ▾ How does overlayfs work?

    It mounts several folders as one: read-only lower layers and one writable
    upper layer. Reading looks through from the top down. The first write to a
    lower file copies the whole file up first ("copy-up"), which is slow for big
    files. Deleting leaves a "whiteout" marker in the upper layer that hides the
    file below. Your mount shows each running container's overlay.

  ▾ What's in an image file?

    An OCI image is a manifest listing layers (tar archives, compressed) and a
    config. Every part is named by its SHA-256 digest, so a registry stores and
    sends each layer once, and pulling an image only downloads the layers you
    don't have yet.

Related
  Commands  docker, podman
  Paths     /var/lib/docker/, /var/lib/containers/
  See also  atfq 7.1 What a container really is
  See also  atfq 13.5 fstab and mount options
